WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.420
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.176
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:16.575
Unit nineteen: World Literature. Today you will balance local and universal ideas with 'while' contrast clauses.

4
00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:19.574
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:23.580
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:25.696
World literature.

7
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:30.297
World literature means literature read beyond its original culture.

8
00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:34.299
World literature courses include writers from every continent.

9
00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:38.255
Your turn. Say: World literature.

10
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.076
World literature.

11
00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:44.945
Circulation.

12
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.877
Circulation means the movement of texts between readers and places.

13
00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:53.655
Online publishing speeds the circulation of stories.

14
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.670
Your turn. Say: Circulation.

15
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:00.918
Circulation.

16
00:01:01.683 --> 00:01:02.754
Translation.

17
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.806
Translation means rendering a work into another language.

18
00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:09.765
Translation gave the novel a second life.

19
00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.478
Your turn. Say: Translation.

20
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:17.358
Translation.

21
00:01:18.117 --> 00:01:19.536
Global readership.

22
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.304
Global readership means readers around the world.

23
00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:25.951
The prize brought a global readership.

24
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.736
Your turn. Say: Global readership.

25
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.054
Global readership.

26
00:01:34.817 --> 00:01:36.456
Local specificity.

27
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:41.621
Local specificity means details belonging to one particular place.

28
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:45.308
Local specificity makes the setting vivid.

29
00:01:45.917 --> 00:01:49.103
Your turn. Say: Local specificity.

30
00:01:51.967 --> 00:01:53.839
Local specificity.

31
00:01:54.583 --> 00:01:56.037
Universal theme.

32
00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:00.454
Universal theme means an idea meaningful in every culture.

33
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.363
Love and loss are universal themes.

34
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.695
Your turn. Say: Universal theme.

35
00:02:09.533 --> 00:02:10.813
Universal theme.

36
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:12.447
Reception.

37
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.866
Reception means how a work is received by readers.

38
00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.624
Its reception abroad was very warm.

39
00:02:20.217 --> 00:02:22.706
Your turn. Say: Reception.

40
00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:26.533
Reception.

41
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:28.702
Canon formation.

42
00:02:29.717 --> 00:02:33.284
Canon formation means the process by which works become 'classics'.

43
00:02:33.883 --> 00:02:36.301
Prizes play a role in canon formation.

44
00:02:36.917 --> 00:02:39.848
Your turn. Say: Canon formation.

45
00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:44.212
Canon formation.

46
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.999
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:54.926
Visal, why do you think the Korean novel we read became popular in so many countries?

48
00:02:56.017 --> 00:03:03.601
While the setting is very local, a small fishing village, the theme of family sacrifice feels universal.

49
00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:07.239
Didn't some details get lost in translation?

50
00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:17.237
Certainly. While the translator kept the local foods and customs, some of the humour depends on wordplay that couldn't survive.

51
00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:22.126
So is local specificity a strength or a weakness abroad?

52
00:03:23.217 --> 00:03:32.220
A strength, I'd say. While readers might not know the place, precise details make the emotions feel real rather than generic.

53
00:03:33.367 --> 00:03:34.832
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:35.683 --> 00:03:38.716
Grammar focus: While plus contrast clause.

55
00:03:39.016 --> 00:03:40.168
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:40.883 --> 00:03:44.079
While the setting is local, the theme feels universal.

57
00:03:44.983 --> 00:03:48.875
While the translator kept local customs, some humour was lost.

58
00:03:49.783 --> 00:03:53.675
While readers might not know the place, they recognise the emotions.

59
00:03:54.633 --> 00:04:04.626
'While' (= although / whereas) introduces a contrast and is common in academic writing. Use a comma after the 'while' clause and do not add 'but'. 'Whilst' is a more formal British variant.

60
00:04:05.383 --> 00:04:10.741
Be careful! Don't say: While the setting is local, but the theme is universal.

61
00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.255
Say: While the setting is local, the theme is universal.

62
00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:18.392
Do not add 'but' after 'while'.

63
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.934
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:22.733 --> 00:04:24.907
Local Stories, Global Readers.

65
00:04:25.207 --> 00:04:30.237
World literature is often defined not by where a work is written but by how widely it circulates.

66
00:04:31.233 --> 00:04:37.412
While some texts travel easily, others remain unknown outside their language because they are never translated.

67
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.801
Paradoxically, works rich in local specificity often reach the widest global readership.

68
00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:52.326
Readers abroad may miss allusions, while still responding deeply to universal themes such as love, injustice and memory.

69
00:04:53.333 --> 00:04:59.385
Prizes, publishers and university syllabi all influence which of these works enter the global canon.

70
00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:04.560
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:05.417 --> 00:05:07.242
Choose the correct sentence.

72
00:05:07.542 --> 00:05:15.735
A, While the plot is simple, but the style is rich.. B, While the plot is simple, the style is rich.. C, While simple, but the style is rich..

73
00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.600
The answer is B: While the plot is simple, the style is rich..

74
00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:26.852
Details belonging to one particular place show blank.

75
00:05:27.152 --> 00:05:32.141
A, universal theme. B, circulation. C, local specificity.

76
00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:38.826
The answer is C: local specificity.

77
00:05:39.983 --> 00:05:42.691
The process by which works become classics is blank.

78
00:05:42.991 --> 00:05:47.724
A, canon formation. B, reception. C, translation.

79
00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:53.916
The answer is A: canon formation.

80
00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:56.991
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:57.291 --> 00:06:03.534
A, reach a global readership. B, arrive a global readership. C, get to readership global.

82
00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.075
The answer is A: reach a global readership.

83
00:06:11.217 --> 00:06:14.714
According to the reading, which works often reach the widest readership?

84
00:06:15.014 --> 00:06:21.535
A, those with no local detail. B, those rich in local specificity. C, those written in English.

85
00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:28.586
The answer is B: those rich in local specificity.

86
00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:32.859
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:33.717 --> 00:06:37.458
What is a universal theme you've found in literature from another culture?

88
00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:55.715
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:56.015 --> 00:07:27.956
A universal theme I found in a Japanese novel was the tension between duty to one's family and personal dreams. While the setting was a small town in Japan, the main character's struggle reminded me of many young Cambodians who feel the same pressure. For example, she wanted to study art, but her parents expected her to run the family shop. Some readers might focus on the cultural differences. I think, however, that the shared emotion is what makes the story powerful.

90
00:07:28.256 --> 00:07:29.849
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:35.666
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and while plus contrast clause.

92
00:07:35.966 --> 00:07:41.033
Today's value is global perspective. Local stories can carry universal truths.

93
00:07:41.333 --> 00:07:43.997
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

94
00:07:44.297 --> 00:07:47.937
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

